    releases</a>.</em></p>
    <blockquote>
    <h2>v27.2.7</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: <code>addResource</code>, and with it <code>saveMissing</code>
    writing to the store, keeps a key below a number, a boolean or an
    array's <code>length</code> flat, like it already did below a string,
    instead of throwing <code>Cannot create property</code> or <code>Invalid
    array length</code>. A request to <code>i18next-http-middleware</code>'s
    <code>missingKeyHandler</code> with
    <code>{&quot;a&quot;:5,&quot;a.b&quot;:&quot;x&quot;}</code> got a 500
    from that throw.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.6</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: an empty plural default value counts, like <code>defaultValue:
    ''</code>: <code>t('key', { count: 0, defaultValue_zero: '',
    defaultValue_other: '{{count}} items' })</code> returns <code>''</code>
    instead of &quot;0 items&quot;, and saveMissing saves <code>''</code>
    for a form given as <code>''</code> (<code>key_zero</code> too) instead
    of another form's text. A form without its own default saves
    <code>defaultValue</code>, then the <code>other</code> default, instead
    of the default resolved for the current count. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/arba-octo"><code>@​arba-octo</code></a> (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2480">#2480</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.5</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix(types): <code>LoggerModule</code>'s <code>log</code>,
    <code>warn</code> and <code>error</code> take one array, the way i18next
    calls them (<code>log(args)</code>); they were typed with rest
    parameters, so a logger typed to spread <code>...args</code> got the
    array as its only argument. Loggers typed either way still compile.</li>
    <li>fix(types): <code>I18nFormatModule</code> declares the members
    i18next calls: <code>init</code>, <code>parse</code>,
    <code>addLookupKeys</code>, <code>handleAsObject</code> and
    <code>getResource</code>, all optional. It declared only
    <code>type</code>, so an object-literal i18n format with these members
    failed to compile.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.4</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix(types): <code>BackendModule.create</code> takes the parameters
    i18next passes it: a backend with <code>callbackApi: true</code> gets
    <code>create(languages, namespace, key, fallbackValue, callback,
    options)</code>, one with <code>callbackApi: false</code> gets
    <code>create(languages, namespace, key, fallbackValue, options)</code>
    and may return a promise. The types declared only the first four
    parameters, so a required fifth or sixth parameter failed to compile and
    an object-literal backend could not take them at all. One rare case now
    fails: a <code>create: (...args) =&gt; other.create(...args)</code> that
    forwards to a <code>create</code> typed with exactly four parameters.
    The doc comments of <code>read</code> and <code>readMulti</code> now
    describe their promise form (<code>read(language, namespace)</code>
    returning the data or a promise of it), which the types already
    accepted.</li>
    <li>fix(types): <code>getFixedT()</code> takes its fourth parameter,
    <code>{ scopeNs }</code>, the full list of namespaces the returned
    <code>t</code> is bound to, which selector keys use to find a namespace
    prefix; lookups still use <code>ns</code> (react-i18next's
    <code>useTranslation</code> passes it).</li>
    <li>fix(types): the doc comment of <code>saveMissingPlurals</code> says
    <code>@default true</code>, the runtime default since the option came in
    10.3.0, not <code>false</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.3</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: a variable in the options of a nested <code>$t(key, {
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;{{name}}&quot; })</code> is escaped once. With
    the default <code>escapeValue: true</code> it was escaped for HTML
    before the options were parsed and again by the nested <code>t()</code>,
    so <code>Tom &amp; Jerry</code> came out as <code>Tom &amp;amp;amp;
    Jerry</code> (and react-i18next's <code>Trans</code> has shown <code>Tom
    &amp;amp; Jerry</code> since 17.1.10, with <code>escapeValue:
    false</code> too). These variables are now escaped for the options'
    JSON, whatever <code>escapeValue</code> is, and the nested
    <code>t()</code> escapes them like the values of a direct call;
    <code>defaultValue</code> and <code>joinArrays</code> there, which it
    does not interpolate, stay escaped once. A value with
    <code>&quot;</code>, <code>'</code>, <code>\</code> or a line break no
    longer breaks the options, with <code>escapeValue: false</code> a value
    can no longer inject options such as <code>lng</code>, and with
    <code>skipOnVariables</code> a <code>{{placeholder}}</code> inside a
    value is no longer interpolated a second time. The warning added in
    26.0.6 now covers <code>{{- var}}</code> in these options, the one form
    still inserted unescaped, instead of <code>escapeValue:
    false</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.2</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: <code>setDefaultNamespace()</code> works on a clone. A clone's
    translator keeps its own options object, so the call changed
    <code>i18n.options.defaultNS</code> while <code>t()</code> kept using
    the old default namespace. i18next-http-middleware hands every request a
    clone, so <code>req.i18n.setDefaultNamespace()</code> did nothing there.
    (<a
    href="https://github.com/i18next/i18next/discussions/2106">#2106</a>)</li>
    <li>fix: <code>supportedLngs</code> works in any casing. Every other
    language code is formatted (<code>pt-br</code> to <code>pt-BR</code>,
    <code>EN</code> to <code>en</code>, all lowercase with
    <code>lowerCaseLng</code>), but <code>supportedLngs</code> was compared
    as written, so <code>supportedLngs: ['pt-br']</code>,
    <code>['EN']</code>, or <code>['pt-BR']</code> with <code>lowerCaseLng:
    true</code> refused that language and fell back. They are now compared
    in that formatted form (also by <code>loadLanguages()</code>), while
    <code>options.supportedLngs</code> stays as written. (<a
    href="https://github.com/i18next/i18next/discussions/1791">#1791</a>)</li>
    <li>fix(types): resources typed with named <code>interface</code>s get
    their nested keys. The key types only walked into objects with an index
    signature, which a type alias has and an interface does not, so
    <code>t('nested.key')</code> failed with TS2345 while
    <code>t('nested')</code> compiled. Interfaces now give the same keys as
    type aliases, so <code>t('nested')</code> without
    <code>returnObjects</code> is rejected for them too. (<a
    href="https://github.com/i18next/i18next/discussions/2326">#2326</a>)</li>
    <li>fix(types): typed keys work with <code>strictNullChecks:
    false</code>. Without strict null checks <code>undefined</code> passes
    <code>extends string</code>, so the key prefix, namespace and context
    checks took a missing <code>keyPrefix</code>, <code>ns</code> or
    <code>context</code> for a value: <code>t('title')</code> was rejected
    (only <code>t('ns:title')</code> compiled), <code>t()</code> returned
    <code>string</code>, and react-i18next's <code>Trans</code> accepted no
    <code>i18nKey</code>. A new type test project compiles with
    <code>strictNullChecks: false</code>; with strict null checks the types
    resolve as before. (<a
    href="https://github.com/i18next/i18next/discussions/2085">#2085</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.1</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: a variable passed as <code>undefined</code> renders as an empty
    string when its placeholder has a format too (<code>{{amount,
    number}}</code>), as <code>{{amount}}</code> already did. The
    placeholder stayed in the result with the default <code>skipOnVariables:
    true</code>, and debug mode logged &quot;missed to pass in
    variable&quot; with either setting. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/Vineet829"><code>@​Vineet829</code></a> (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2479">#2479</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.2.0</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: an error thrown by a load callback or a listener (the callback
    of <code>init</code>, <code>changeLanguage</code>,
    <code>loadNamespaces</code> or <code>loadLanguages</code>, or a
    <code>languageChanged</code> / <code>loaded</code> listener) is no
    longer handed back to the backend connector as if the read had failed.
    With a backend whose <code>read</code> returns a value or a promise, the
    connector got that error a second time: the namespace that had loaded
    was marked as failed, <code>failedLoading</code> was emitted with your
    error and the count of running reads dropped to -1. The error now
    reaches your app: it is thrown from the call that started the load for a
    <code>read</code> that returns a value, and it is an unhandled rejection
    for a <code>read</code> that returns a promise, which ends a Node.js
    process unless you handle <code>unhandledRejection</code>. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/SudeepGowda55"><code>@​SudeepGowda55</code></a>
    (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2477">#2477</a>)</li>
    <li>fix: when a load callback throws, the other callbacks waiting for
    the same namespaces are still called and the <code>loaded</code> event
    is still emitted, and the error is thrown on after that. A second
    <code>loadNamespaces()</code> for the same namespace never called back
    before.</li>
    <li>fix: <code>addResourceBundle(lng, ns, resources, true, true)</code>
    overwrites numbers and booleans too. Only strings were replaced, so
    <code>{ n: 0 }</code> stayed 0 after adding <code>{ n: 5 }</code>, and
    adding an object where the bundle had a number, boolean or null threw
    <code>Cannot create property ... on number</code>, also without
    <code>overwrite</code>. With <code>overwrite</code>, a number, boolean
    or null now also replaces a nested object, as a string already did.
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/SudeepGowda55"><code>@​SudeepGowda55</code></a>
    (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2476">#2476</a>)</li>
    <li>fix: with <code>keySeparator: false</code>, a namespace separator
    inside the key after the namespace is kept.
    <code>t('common:errors:required')</code> looked up
    <code>'errorsfalserequired'</code> instead of the flat key
    <code>'errors:required'</code> that i18next-cli extracts (also for a
    selector like <code>$ =&gt; $.common['errors:required']</code>), and
    reported the missing key under that name. This dates back to 11.3.
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/SudeepGowda55"><code>@​SudeepGowda55</code></a>
    (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2478">#2478</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.1.5</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix: <code>off(event, listener)</code> removes a listener added with
    <code>once()</code>. <code>once()</code> registers a wrapper, so
    <code>off()</code> with the listener itself found nothing and the
    listener still ran on the next event. A <code>once()</code> listener is
    now also removed before it runs, so one that emits the same event again
    (like calling <code>changeLanguage()</code> from
    <code>languageChanged</code>) or throws runs only once. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/SudeepGowda55"><code>@​SudeepGowda55</code></a>
    (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2475">#2475</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.1.4</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>fix(types): with <code>enableSelector</code>, assigning a
    <code>t</code> function to a wider <code>TFunction</code>
    (<code>TFunction&lt;'ns'&gt;</code> to <code>TFunction&lt;'ns',
    string&gt;</code> or <code>TFunction&lt;Namespace&gt;</code>, or a key
    prefix to a wider one) failed with TS2590 &quot;Expression produces a
    union type that is too complex to represent&quot; once the namespaces
    had about 600 top-level keys together. This started in 26.3.3. Selector
    results for JSON arrays that mix arrays with objects or strings also
    keep the inner arrays as arrays. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/reckter"><code>@​reckter</code></a> (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2474">#2474</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.1.3</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>security: a language code named after a property every object
    inherits (<code>hasOwnProperty</code>, <code>isPrototypeOf</code>,
    <code>toLocaleString</code>, <code>__defineGetter__</code>, ...) made
    the backend connector throw <code>obj[k].push is not a function</code>
    when its load finished. A language detector passes such a code on from a
    request (<code>?lng=hasOwnProperty</code>), and with an asynchronous
    backend nothing caught the throw, so one request ended a Node.js server
    process (in a browser, <code>init()</code> never resolved). Now these
    codes load like any other unknown language. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/superbot-helper"><code>@​superbot-helper</code></a>
    (<a
    href="https://github.com/i18next/i18next/security/advisories/GHSA-q76c-gcvw-89x9">GHSA-q76c-gcvw-89x9</a>)</li>
    <li>fix: with an object <code>fallbackLng</code>, a language code named
    like an <code>Object.prototype</code> property
    (<code>constructor</code>, <code>toString</code>,
    <code>hasOwnProperty</code>, <code>constructor-US</code>) gets the
    <code>default</code> fallback instead of making <code>t()</code>,
    <code>exists()</code>, <code>getFixedT()</code> and
    <code>changeLanguage()</code> throw <code>fallbackCodes.forEach is not a
    function</code>. Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/cyphercodes"><code>@​cyphercodes</code></a> (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2473">#2473</a>,
    <a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/issues/2472">#2472</a>)</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.1.2</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>security: language codes and namespaces that come from requests no
    longer grow the memory of a server without end. The backend connector
    kept one entry per failed load, so that it does not read it again, and
    the language hierarchy and plural rule caches kept one entry per
    language code. With the default <code>supportedLngs: false</code>, every
    made-up language a detector found (<code>?lng=xx1</code>,
    <code>?lng=xx2</code>, ...) or every made-up namespace requested from
    i18next-http-middleware's <code>getResourcesHandler</code> added about
    180 bytes for good. Now only the most recent 1000 failed loads are kept
    (an older one is read again when it is needed), and both caches are
    emptied at 1000 codes, as the formatted language codes already were.
    Pairs with i18next-http-middleware 3.10.1 (<a
    href="https://github.com/i18next/i18next-http-middleware/security/advisories/GHSA-mjcw-gfh6-3cxm">GHSA-mjcw-gfh6-3cxm</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>v27.1.1</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>perf: <code>t()</code> skips the interpolation and nesting regexp
    scans when the text has no interpolation or nesting prefix, about 6-14%
    faster for short texts and up to 75% for long ones. The output does not
    change. Thanks <a href="https://github.com/g4lb"><code>@​g4lb</code></a>
    (<a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/i18next/i18next/pull/2469">#2469</a>)</li>
    </ul>
